// # Callees between a reference's acquisition and its drop
//
// Card REFCOUNT-FAMILY-BLOCKING-CALLEES. Rule 13b withdraws a reference
// family at a refused statement that may hand its name on, so a drop after a
// call to an uncontracted callee stays unheld. The card ranked, for every
// declared family the corpus withdrew, the statements that withdrew it
// (`docs/reports/refcount-family-blocking-callees/`). The callees below are
// the members of those withdrawer sets that the vocabulary on main states
// exactly: each body was read at `66910c10` and `046accf6` (byte-identical,
// `scripts/body.py`), none keeps, drops, retains or moves a reference on the
// object it is handed, and each is pinned (C06.2). Callees that keep a
// reference on their argument into an owner they build (an op, a reply
// queue, a forwarding queue) or take a C structure by value are not declared
// (`W48-CALLEE-DECISION-TABLE` class 3, `REFCOUNT-BY-VALUE-AGGREGATE-ABI`).
//
// THE SERVED-QUEUE PREMISE (a declared assumption, constraint 4).
// `rd_kafka_q_wait_result` (through `rd_kafka_q_pop`) and `rd_kafka_q_purge0`
// may run a popped op's own callback (`rd_kafka_op_handle` -> `rd_kafka_op_call`,
// `rd_kafka_op_destroy`'s `RD_KAFKA_RESP_ERR__DESTROY` call), which receives
// the served queue or the op: the declarations assume such a callback takes
// over no reference the caller holds on that queue. The callbacks themselves
// are not modelled (`CG22-CALLBACK-REGISTRY-CONTRACT`); `rd_kafka_q_serve*`
// and `rd_kafka_q_pop_serve*`, which take a callback argument, stay
// undeclared.
//
// Card VERDICT-CONTRACT-DEPENDENCIES: both declarations state the premise as
// `requires resource_premise(served_queue, "...")` (contract spec C04.18), so
// every verdict that rests on either names it; the review evidence is
// `docs/reports/refcount-family-blocking-callees/README.md` section 9.

// ## Waiting on and purging a queue
//
// `rd_kafka_q_pop` (`rdkafka_queue.c:537-540`) is deliberately NOT declared
// here, although its body states exactly (`rkq` borrowed, the popped op an
// owned nullable result): measured declared, it turned ten Unknown rows into
// leak candidates, one of them on an infeasible path (`rd_kafka_metadata`
// reaches its loop head again only when `err == RD_KAFKA_RESP_ERR__DESTROY_BROKER`,
// after destroying the op; the route does not correlate the two tests of
// `err`), and it repairs no reference family (`REFCOUNT-Q-POP-OWNED-OP`).
//
// `rd_kafka_q_pop(rkq, timeout_us, version)` is
// `rd_kafka_q_pop_serve(rkq, timeout_us, version, RD_KAFKA_Q_CB_RETURN, NULL,
// NULL)` (`:507-515`), i.e. the static `rd_kafka_q_pop_serve0` (`:398-505`):
// under `rkq->rkq_lock` it either takes the forward queue
// (`rd_kafka_q_fwd_get`, `rdkafka_queue.h:286-299`, which keeps
// `rkq->rkq_fwdq`), pops from it recursively and drops it again
// (`rd_kafka_q_destroy(fwdq)`): another object, balanced; or it dequeues ops
// (`rd_kafka_q_deq0`), lets `rd_kafka_op_handle` handle each (a handled op is
// destroyed there) and returns the first op handed back, or NULL on a yield
// or when the absolute timeout passes. `rkq` is only locked, read and
// dequeued from.
//
// `rd_kafka_q_wait_result(rkq, timeout_ms)` (`rdkafka_queue.c:1199-1212`):
// `rko = rd_kafka_q_pop(rkq, rd_timeout_us(timeout_ms), 0)`; NULL gives
// `RD_KAFKA_RESP_ERR__TIMED_OUT`, otherwise the op's `rko_err` is read and the
// op destroyed (`rd_kafka_op_destroy`). It returns that error code and keeps
// nothing: `rkq` is borrowed. The result is `rd_kafka_resp_err_t`, `i32`
// (rule 12u, see the head of this module).

extern fn rd_kafka_q_wait_result(rkq: ptr<mut, u8>, timeout_ms: i32) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_premise(served_queue, "an op callback the call may run (rd_kafka_op_handle, rd_kafka_op_call, rd_kafka_op_destroy) takes over no reference the caller holds on the served queue");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(rkq);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(timeout_ms);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// `rd_kafka_q_purge0(rkq, do_lock)` (`rdkafka_queue.c:205-243`; the
// `rd_kafka_q_purge` macro, `rdkafka_queue.h:208`, passes `1`): with a forward
// queue, purges that (`rd_kafka_q_fwd_get` keeps it, `rd_kafka_q_purge(fwdq)`,
// `rd_kafka_q_destroy(fwdq)`: balanced) and returns its count; otherwise moves
// the queue's ops to a local list (`TAILQ_MOVE`), marks the queue served,
// resets its list and counters (`rd_kafka_q_reset`, `rdkafka_queue.h:263-268`)
// and destroys each moved op (`rd_kafka_op_destroy`, which drops what the op
// held, not the caller's). `rkq` itself is never kept, dropped or stored:
// borrowed. The result is the number of ops purged.

extern fn rd_kafka_q_purge0(rkq: ptr<mut, u8>, do_lock: i32) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_premise(served_queue, "an op callback the call may run (rd_kafka_op_handle, rd_kafka_op_call, rd_kafka_op_destroy) takes over no reference the caller holds on the served queue");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(rkq);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(do_lock);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

